            "Certainly. A comet is mostly made of ice and dust, with a nucleus that releases gas and dust, forming a glowing coma and sometimes a tail when it comes close to the sun. An asteroid, on the other hand, is a rocky body that orbits the sun but does not typically release material as a comet does.",
            "Not all comets develop tails. The tail forms when a comet's orbit brings it close enough to the sun for its ice to start vaporizing. It's the sun's radiation pressure and solar wind that then blow the dust and gas away from the comet’s nucleus, creating the tail. Comets that are farther from the sun or have less volatile materials may not exhibit visible tails.",
            "Yes, some asteroids can be seen from Earth. Usually, they are visible through telescopes as small points of light. However, occasionally, a larger asteroid will pass close enough to Earth and may be visible to the naked eye or with the aid of binoculars. Unlike comets, asteroids do not have tails and appear as steady points of light moving across the sky."
