        "ref_answers": [
            "The theory of relativity, proposed by Albert Einstein, revolutionizes our perception of space and time by describing them as a single, four-dimensional spacetime continuum. It shows that the measurements of time and space differ for observers in different states of motion. According to the theory's special relativity portion, as an object moves faster, time for that object slows down relative to a stationary observer. Furthermore, general relativity explains how massive objects can distort spacetime, affecting the passage of time and the path of light. This means that time and space are not absolute; they are fluid concepts that can be stretched and shrunk by motion and gravity.",
            "Absolutely! So imagine space and time are like a big trampoline. Now, if you put something heavy on it, like a bowling ball, it'll make a dent. That's what big stuff like planets and stars do to space. This dent changes the way things move around it—like if you rolled a marble close to the bowling ball, it would start to go around in a curve instead of a straight line. Now, here's where it gets wild. If you're running really fast on this trampoline, time for you actually goes slower than for someone who's just chilling on the grass. So, if you could run close to the speed of light, something that takes you just a minute might take years for someone not moving. That's what Einstein's theory of relativity is all about—space and time are stretchy and can bend and slow down because of heavy stuff and super-fast speeds. Cool, huh?"
        ],
