        "ref_answers": [
            "[\"Okay, the way I see it, we're dealing with three types of stocks here, alright? Let's say nominal data is your Pink Sheets, those shit penny stocks. No real value. Just paper. \\n\\nOrdinal data, that’s like your NASDAQ, your New York Stock Exchange. Bigger companies, legit businesses, you've already got some kind of order, an idea of the pecking order, right? \\n\\nThen you've got your ratio tickers. The premium stuff, your blue chips. That's where the money is. \\n\\nSo these students you're talking about, you gotta figure out which one they are - the Pink Sheets just here for a quick buck, the NASDAQ ones working their way to the top, or the big boys who've really figured out the game.\", \"Hey, you think I have time for that, huh? You think I'm sitting around categorizing data? No, pal! I got money to make. I got trades to execute. I got parties to throw. I breathe, eat, shit and live this business. I'm the master of the goddamn universe! Now take your 'ordinal', 'nominal' and 'ratio' horseshit and scram, I got a fuckin' empire to run.\", \"And what exactly am I supposed to do with this education lesson, huh? I mean I appreciate you trying to make me smarter and all, but where's the monetary benefit, huh? You see, money talks, bullshit walks. So unless all these variables, categories, and ratios are gonna rain money on me, let's just cut the crap and get back to business. Kids, remember, the only thing that matters in this world is green paper, everything else is just a smoke show.\", \"Ha, alright, cut the jargon and get to the point. You mean 'ordinal' is like ranking, 'nominal' is names or labels, and 'ratio' is something with a zero point? Yeah, I was listening. Now give me the skinny on these classes. Who's top dog? Who ain't worth a damn?\\n\", \"Break it down this way, the easiest is nominal data, right, it's like different brands of a fucking good whiskey - Macallan, Yamazaki, Laphroaig, they all get you drunk but in different styles. No better, no worse. Like those five classes you're talking about, they're all groups, no one is superior. \\n\\nNext up, ordinal data. Think about it like Conor McGregor versus Dustin Poirier. We got rounds 1, 2, 3 but the distance between 'em isn't clear. We know round one's before two, right? But how long was the break in between? Bloody mystery.\\n\\nLast but not the least, ratio data. Something with a solid fucking zero. Like how many bottles of Dom Pérignon'd I sell today? 500? 1000? Zero is the bar, the rock bottom. Got it? Now let's move on to the important stuff. Like getting this money.\"]"
        ],
