            "user_audio_transcripts": [
                "You will be asked to sort data into three types: ordinal, nominal, and ratio. Ordinal data is a type of category where the order matters, but the exact distance between categories is not known. Nominal data is also a category type, but the order does not matter at all. Ratio data includes numbers where both the order and the exact differences matter, and zero means there is none of that thing. For example: \"Five classes with one hundred students each.\""
            ],
            "raw_question": "In this task, you will be asked to categorize data into different types. There are 3 types of data in total and they are 'ordinal', 'nominal' and 'ratio'.\n- Ordinal data is a categorical, statistical data type where the variables have natural, ordered categories and the distances between the categories is not known. \n- Nominal data is a categorical variable that has two or more categories where there is no intrinsic ordering to the categories. \n- Ratio variable has all the properties of an interval variable with the additional property of having a clear definition of 0.0 occurring (thus allowing multiplication).\n5 classes with 100 students each.",
