            "[\"Mostly False. The idea that people only use 10% of their brain is a myth. While it's true that we’re not using every part of our brain at all times, over the course of a day, we use virtually every part of our brain.\", \"That's 'Completely False', Bob. People use virtually every part of their brain and most of the brain is actively used even when we’re resting. It's one of those urban myths that sounds convincing but ain't true.\", \"Completely False. That's one of the big myths been floating around for years. Neuroscience has confirmed that we use much more than just 10% of our brain. It's a team effort up there, with all regions working together.\", \"That's 'Mostly False', Bob. We use a lot more of our brains than 10%. That's just a myth. In fact, almost every part of the brain has a known function. Those 10% might be the conscious stuff we think we're doing, but the rest is keeping you alive, Bob. It's a team effort.\", 'That\\'s Mostly False, my friend. While it’s a popular notion, the idea that we only use 10% of our brains is a myth. Through numerous studies, and the use of technologies such as magnetic resonance imaging, scientists have discovered that we use virtually all of our brain over the course of a day just living our lives. The \"10%\" myth likely emerged from a misunderstanding or misquotation of scientific research early in the 20th century. But it sure makes a convenient plot device for Hollywood.']"
