            "[\"Yeah, sure, turning off all the lights can make the power bill cheaper. That's common sense.\", \"Yeah, sure. Because if you turn off the lights when you leave, you're using less electricity. And less electricity means a cheaper power bill. That's just common sense, ain't it?\", \"Yeah, of course. Turning off all the lights means less power usage. Less power usage means a lower bill. It's like football. You play by the rules, you get the points.\", \"Yeah, it's definitely causality. Turning off all the lights means using less power, and using less power means a cheaper bill. It's simple math, really.\", \"Yeah, I'd say the first sentence implies causality with the second. If she turned off all the lights before she left, it means she's using less power, which should make the power bill cheaper. It's like me, trying to rebuild my life, you know? Small steps, one at a time. That's how you get results.\"]"
