            "['Ha! An amusing predicament. On one hand, you wish to identify those wavering in their loyalties, to sway them back into your fold. On the other, you wish to staunch the flow of deserters from your ranks. You see them as separate, but they are two heads of the same beast. In bolstering your defenses against churn, you court those who might defect. The two objectives are not at odds, but rather feed into each other. They are compatible, in my eyes.', \"Incompatible, they are not! Both objectives, as thou hast put forth, aim at the preservation of customer fidelity. The first seeks to identify those wavering in their loyalty, the second, to reduce the number of those who abandon our brand. Both, in essence, strive to fortify the bond 'twixt us and our customers, and hence, they align. They are not at odds, but rather two strategies marching towards the same end.\", 'These objectives seem to draw from the same vein. To identify those likely to switch brands, to them extend loyalty; and in the same breath, to reduce the customer churn. They are not foes, but allies in this game of loyalty and retention. They require the same action - target those who waver on the edge of loyalty, and pull them back into the fold. They are, in my judgment, compatible.', 'These objectives, they resonate together, not against. Identifying those likely to switch brands and targeting them with loyalty programs is but a strategy to reduce customer churn. They march to the same drum, they do.', 'They seem to be at odds, do they not? If we are to focus our efforts on currying favor with those with wandering eyes, then surely we cannot also be concerned with reducing the number who leave our ranks. We are either chasing potential deserters or securing the loyal. One cannot run in two directions at once.']"
