        "image_3": "",
        "image_4": "",
        "extra": {
            "user_audio_transcripts": [
                "Here is a summary of a research study: When people do two thinking tasks at the same time, the interference can be either domain-general or domain-specific. Domain-general interference happens when there are not enough overall mental resources for both tasks. Domain-specific interference happens when there are not enough resources in a specific area, like vision or movement. Recent studies suggest that language about visual or motor actions might use the same mental systems as actually seeing or moving, through mental simulation. Other studies have shown that people increase their following distance when they are distracted or stressed by weather while driving. Some researchers wanted to see how language about visual, motor, or abstract topics affects driving, specifically reaction times and following distance. In their experiment, all participants spoke English fluently and were trained to use the driving simulator. They had to keep both hands on the wheel. While driving, they answered true or false questions in different language conditions. For example, a visual sentence might be, \"A stop sign is green,\" and the correct answer would be \"false.\" A motor sentence might be, \"It is possible to crush a remote between the thumb and first finger,\" with the answer \"false.\" Abstract sentences were from the U.S. citizenship exam, like, \"The American Civil War took place before World War Two,\" with the answer \"true.\" There was also a control group who just repeated \"true\" or \"false.\" The average following distance for each group is shown in Figure 1. There were significant differences between all the groups. The term \"LBFS accident,\" which stands for \"Look But Failed to See,\" describes an accident where a driver looked but still did not see an oncoming car that should have been visible. Which of the following would NOT explain this type of accident? Option A: Saccadic masking, where the eyes are briefly blind while moving. Option B: Driving on an unfamiliar road. Option C: Domain-general and domain-specific interference. Option D: An experienced driver’s mental model does not match the oncoming car."
            ],
            "raw_question": "The interference resulting from the performance of two simultaneous cognitive tasks can be categorized as either domain-general or domain-specific. Domain-general interference results from a lack of overall cognitive resources to attend to both tasks. A domain-specific interference is related to a lack of resources in a specific system, such as the visual or motor system. Recent research has suggested that language regarding visual and motoric content may engage some of the same systems through mental simulation, rather than engaging separate systems for language and perceptual systems. Studies have shown following distance increases with increased distraction or weather related stress. Other researchers wanted to understand the implications of this research on driving ability. They set up an experiment to test the effects of content specific language involving visual, motor, or abstract content on reaction times and following distance. All participants spoke English fluently and underwent training to ensure competence in the driving simulator did not affect their performance of the task. Participants were required to keep both hands on the wheel during the experiment. After the driving simulation began, the participants had to respond to a battery of true and false questions in each language condition. A possible visual language condition sentence is 'A stop sign is green'; a correct response is speaking the word 'false'. Sentences in the motor condition included fine motor details and differed from required movements for driving (e.g. 'It is possible to crush a remote between the thumb and first finger'; similarly, this would require speaking 'false'). Abstract sentences were taken from the U.S. citizenship exam (e.g. 'The American Civil War took place before WWII'; 'true'). The mean following distance from the pace car for each of the three language conditions and a control group (asked to repeat the word 'true' or 'false') is shown in Figure 1. Figure 1: The mean following distance between the participant's vehicle and the vehicle in front of the participant in the simulation. There were significant differences between pairwise comparisons in all conditions. <image 1> The term 'LBFS (Look But Failed to See) accident' has crept into the nomenclature to describe an accident where the driver looked in a direction, only to be hit by an oncoming vehicle that should have been highly visible from that same direction. Which of these would NOT account for this problem?",
            "raw_options": [
                "Saccadic masking occurrence, in which moving eyes are rendered temporarily blind",
                "Driving on an unfamiliar road",
                "Domain-general and domain-specific interference",
                "An experienced driver's existing schema does not match an oncoming car"
            ],
            "role_name": "",
            "user_audio_emotion": ""
        }
