        "extra": {
            "user_audio_transcripts": [
                "Drug addiction is often linked to criminal behavior, and some people start their prison sentence already addicted to drugs. In these situations, treatment aims to help the person overcome addiction and reduce criminal behavior after they are released. One prison tried to improve their treatment by adding new options to traditional addiction therapy. They offered three types of treatment and wanted to see if these had different long-term results.  The first treatment included both therapy and medication while the person was in prison and after they were released. The second treatment included therapy in prison, but medication only after release. The third treatment included therapy in prison and a referral to a clinic where the person could get medication if they wanted it after release.  People from each group were contacted one year after leaving prison. Researchers then calculated how many people in each group had returned to drug use. Table 1 shows the percentage of people in each group who were addicted to each drug one year after release. Please refer to the image for the data.  Which group was most likely to relapse? A. People addicted to opiates who received medication both before and after release. B. People addicted to cocaine who only received medication referrals. C. People addicted to opiates who received medication only after release. D. People addicted to cocaine who received medication both before and after release."
            ],
            "raw_question": "Drug addiction is often correlated with criminal behavior, and some convicted criminals begin their prison sentence already addicted to drugs. In these cases, treatment is focused on not only helping the person overcome drug addiction, but also reducing criminal behavior upon parole. One prison decided to try to improve their current treatment options by providing additions to traditional addiction psychotherapy. They offered three different types of treatment, and investigated if those treatment types had different long-term outcomes. The first treatment included psychotherapy and medication while the individuals were incarcerated and when they were released on parole. The second treatment included psychotherapy while the individuals were in prison, and medication only once they were released on parole. The third treatment included psychotherapy while the individuals were in prison, and a referral to a clinic where individuals could get medication if they wanted it after they were released. Participants from each group were contacted a year after they were released from prison, and researchers were then able to calculate how many individuals in each treatment condition had returned to drug abuse. Table 1 shows the percentage of people in each condition who were addicted to each drug one year after being released from prison. <image 1> Which group was most susceptible to relapse?",
            "raw_options": [
                "Individuals who were addicted to opiates and received medication both pre and post release.",
                "Individuals who were addicted to cocaine and only received medication referrals.",
                "Individuals who were addicted to opiates and received medication post release only.",
                "Individuals who were addicted to cocaine and received medication both pre and post release."
            ],
            "role_name": "",
            "user_audio_emotion": ""
        }
