    "Viewing_Multi_Discipline_834": {
        "id": "Viewing_Multi_Discipline_834",
        "category1": "Viewing",
        "category2": "Multi_Discipline",
        "system_prompt": "",
        "role_audio": "",
        "sound_audio": "",
        "user_audio_0": "data/examples/Viewing/Multi_Discipline/Viewing_Multi_Discipline_834/user_audio_0.mp3",
        "user_audio_1": "",
        "user_audio_2": "",
        "user_audio_3": "",
        "user_audio_4": "",
        "user_audio_5": "",
        "user_audio_6": "",
        "ref_answers": [
            "C"
        ],
        "image_0": "data/examples/Viewing/Multi_Discipline/Viewing_Multi_Discipline_834/image_0.jpg",
        "image_1": "",
        "image_2": "",
        "image_3": "",
        "image_4": "",
        "extra": {
            "user_audio_transcripts": [
                "Researchers have studied how people remember and forget information. In a famous set of experiments, the psychologist Hermann Ebbinghaus looked at how people store and recall information. He created something called the \"forgetting curve,\" which shows how quickly people forget what they have learned. In one study, participants learned a list of items on the first day. Researchers then measured how much of the list they remembered as time passed. On the second day, some participants learned the list again, and their memory was measured over time. This process was repeated on the third and fourth days, with some participants relearning the list each day. The results showed different forgetting curves, with the amount remembered on the y-axis and the time in days on the x-axis. Based on these forgetting curves, which statement best describes how forgetting happens? Option A: Most forgetting happens slowly over a long time. Option B: Most forgetting happens after a few days. Option C: Most forgetting happens soon after learning. Option D: Most forgetting happens at a steady rate over time."
            ],
            "raw_question": "Researchers have long studied human memory. In a classic series of studies, the German psychologist Hermann Ebbinghaus investigated the storage and recall of information in memory. Based on his findings, he developed the so-called forgetting curve, a way of illustrating the rate at which people forget the information they have learned. The forgetting curve has been studied in a variety of different environments and for a variety of different stimuli. On day 1 of the memory research study, participants were asked to learn a list of items. Researchers then tracked the proportion of the list that the participants remembered as time passed. On day 2, some participants were asked to relearn the list. Again, the proportion remembered was tracked over time. On day 3, some participants were asked to relearn the list a second time, and the proportion they remembered was tracked over time. On day 4, some participants were asked to relearn the list a third time, and the proportion they remembered was tracked over time. The data from this study produced the following set of forgetting curves. Each line represents the memory of the learned or relearned list. The proportion of the list remembered is shown on the y-axis (memory), and the time interval for forgetting is shown on the x-axis (time remembered in days). <image 1> Based on the graph, which of the following statements BEST characterizes forgetting?",
            "raw_options": [
                "Most forgetting occurs gradually over a long period of time.",
                "Most forgetting occurs after only a few days.",
                "Most forgetting occurs soon after information is learned.",
                "Most forgetting occurs at a constant rate over time."
            ],
            "role_name": "",
            "user_audio_emotion": ""
        }
    },
    "Viewing_Multi_Discipline_835": {
        "id": "Viewing_Multi_Discipline_835",
        "category1": "Viewing",
        "category2": "Multi_Discipline",
        "system_prompt": "",
        "role_audio": "",
        "sound_audio": "",
        "user_audio_0": "data/examples/Viewing/Multi_Discipline/Viewing_Multi_Discipline_835/user_audio_0.mp3",
        "user_audio_1": "",
        "user_audio_2": "",
        "user_audio_3": "",
        "user_audio_4": "",
        "user_audio_5": "",
        "user_audio_6": "",
        "ref_answers": [
            "C"
        ],
        "image_0": "data/examples/Viewing/Multi_Discipline/Viewing_Multi_Discipline_835/image_0.jpg",
        "image_1": "",
        "image_2": "",
        "image_3": "",
        "image_4": "",
        "extra": {
            "user_audio_transcripts": [
                "As you sleep, your brain and body go through different stages. These include changes in brain wave activity, eye movement, muscle tone, and body temperature. There are four main stages: N1, N2, N3, and REM. REM stands for rapid eye movement. This stage is where you have your most vivid dreams and is important for feeling rested. During REM, your muscles are relaxed because the motor neurons are inhibited. N1, N2, and N3 are called non-REM sleep stages, or synchronized sleep, because of the patterns seen on EEG recordings. N1 is the lightest stage and is when you might feel like you are falling and experience \"hypnic jerks.\" N2 is light sleep and makes up about half of your night. N3 is deep sleep, with very slow brain waves, and is when sleepwalking usually happens. As you fall asleep, you move through N1, N2, N3, then back to N2, and finally into REM sleep. You usually reach REM sleep about 90 minutes after falling asleep, and the first REM period lasts about 20 minutes. As the night goes on, you spend more time in REM sleep and less in deep sleep. On average, you go through five of these sleep cycles each night. Now, look at Figure 1. How many complete sleep cycles can you see in Figure 1? Is it A, 7; B, 5; C, 4; or D, 6?"
            ],
            "raw_question": "As you sleep, your brain and body cycle through varying levels of brain wave activity and eye movement; even muscle tone and core body temperature change throughout the night. These levels can be broken down into four major stages: N1, N2, N3, and REM. REM refers to rapid eye movement, a major characteristic of this stage. REM is the stage where your most vivid dreams occur and is required to feel fully rested. It is also characterized by atonia caused by inhibition of the motor neurons. N1, N2, and N3 are all non-rapid-eye-movement sleep stages, also called synchronized sleep, due to the nature of electroencephalogram (EEG) recordings during these periods. N1 is the onset of sleep, and when you experience \"hypnic jerks\" - often associated with the feeling of falling. N2, light sleep, is where you spend about 50% of your night. N3, known as deep sleep, is characterized by very slow brain waves. Sleepwalking, also known as somnambulism, occurs chiefly during this sleep stage. As you fall asleep, you pass through N1, N2, N3, and back to N2 before achieving REM sleep. This typically occurs about 90 minutes after falling asleep, and the first REM will last about 20 minutes. As the night progresses, one spends more time in REM sleep, and less in N3. The average night of sleep includes five of these cycles. <image 1> How many complete sleep cycles can be seen in Figure 1?",
            "raw_options": [
                "7",
                "5",
                "4",
                "6"
            ],
            "role_name": "",
            "user_audio_emotion": ""
        }
    },
